---
import { S } from "../lib/state";
import Service from "../layouts/Service.astro";
import Triage from "../components/Triage.astro";
---
<Service
  title={`Water damage restoration in ${S.name}`}
  seoTitle={`Water Damage Restoration in ${S.name} | Burst Pipes, Basements`}
  description={`Burst pipe, flooded basement or sewage backup in ${S.name}? Get connected with a local water damage restoration company, plus step-by-step guidance while you wait.`}
  eyebrow="Water damage"
  lede="Burst pipes, flooded basements, leaking water heaters and sewage backups. Get connected with a restoration company that serves your county, and use the steps below to limit the damage now."
  path="/water-damage/"
  name="Water damage restoration"
  metric="freeze"
  process={[
    ["Inspection", "Find the source, stop it, map how far water traveled with moisture meters and thermal cameras, and assign a category and class."],
    ["Extraction", "Pump out standing water and pull water out of carpet and pad with truck-mounted or portable extractors."],
    ["Drying", "Set air movers and dehumidifiers sized to the space, and take moisture readings daily until materials reach dry standard."],
    ["Cleaning and repair", "Clean and treat affected areas, remove materials that can't be saved, then rebuild drywall, trim and flooring."],
  ]}
  guides={[
    { href: "/guides/water-damage-first-hour/", label: "The first hour", note: "Safety, shutoff, photos and drying" },
    { href: "/guides/water-damage-categories/", label: "Clean, gray or black water", note: "Why the category changes the job" },
    { href: `/guides/${S.slug}-water-damage-insurance-claim/`, label: `${S.name} insurance claims`, note: "Coverage and your insurer's deadlines" },
  ]}
  faq={[
    { q: "What should I do first after water damage?", a: "Make it safe (stay out of water near electricity, and leave if you smell gas), stop the water at the fixture valve or the main shutoff, photograph everything before cleanup, then call your insurer and a restoration company. Drying should start within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold." },
    { q: "How long does water damage restoration take?", a: "Drying a typical clean-water job takes about three to five days with professional equipment. Hardwood, plaster and concrete take longer. Repairs such as new drywall, paint and flooring come after drying and can add days to weeks." },
    { q: `Does homeowners insurance cover water damage in ${S.name}?`, a: "Usually, if the water was sudden and accidental, such as a burst pipe or a failed supply line. Standard policies usually exclude slow leaks, flooding from outside, and sewer backups without an endorsement. Your policy language decides." },
    { q: "Can I dry water damage myself?", a: "A small, clean spill on a hard floor caught right away usually can be. Once water has soaked carpet pad, gone under baseboards or wicked up drywall, professional extraction, air movers, dehumidifiers and moisture readings are the reliable way to dry inside walls and floors." },
  ]}
>
  <section class="section" slot="before">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
      <h2>What's happening?</h2>
      <Triage />
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Mitigation vs. restoration</h2>
  <p>You'll hear two words. <strong>Mitigation</strong> is the emergency part: stopping the water, extracting it and drying the structure so damage doesn't spread. <strong>Restoration</strong> or reconstruction is putting the house back: new drywall, paint, flooring and cabinets. Some companies do both. Others only mitigate and hand off to a contractor. Ask which you're getting.</p>
  <h2>How long drying takes</h2>
  <p>A typical clean-water job takes three to five days of drying equipment running, longer for hardwood, plaster or a finished basement. A good company records moisture readings every day and removes equipment only when readings show the materials are dry, not on a fixed schedule.</p>
  <h2>Choosing a company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask whether technicians are IICRC certified in water damage restoration.</li>
    <li>Ask for a written scope and an estimate before non-emergency work starts.</li>
    <li>Ask whether they bill your insurer directly, and what you'll owe.</li>
    <li>Read anything you sign. Know whether it's a work authorization or an assignment of benefits.</li>
  </ul>
</Service>
